    "content": "\n      <h2>1. The Complete 7-Phase Intelligence Lifecycle for Sherlock</h2>\n      <p>Mastering Sherlock requires embedding technical commands into a structured, repeatable analytical framework. Professional intelligence teams follow the 7-Phase Lifecycle:</p>\n      Preparation -> Collection -> Validation -> Correlation -> Preservation -> Analysis -> Reporting.\n      <p>By moving systematically through each phase of this lifecycle, analysts avoid chaotic, unstructured querying and ensure that every collection action produces verifiable, defensible intelligence deliverables.</p>\n\n      <h3>2. Critical Limitations and Operational Boundaries</h3>\n      <p>Cannot verify private account contents or bypass platform login walls.</p>\n      <p>Understanding what Sherlock <em>cannot</em> do is just as important as knowing its strengths. Operating outside its technical capabilities leads to wasted investigative effort, false sense of security, and flawed intelligence deliverables.</p>\n      <p>When operating in high-stakes threat intelligence or legal investigations, analysts must explicitly document known limitations and confidence caveats within the intelligence brief (e.g., noting that registrant data reflects proxy masking or that active DNS records may fluctuate due to dynamic GeoDNS routing).</p>\n\n      <h3>3. Common Investigation Mistakes & Analytical Traps</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Over-Reliance on Single Data Points:</strong> Treating a single unverified record as definitive proof without corroborating evidence.</li>\n        <li><strong>Ignoring Cache and Propagation Delays:</strong> Failing to account for TTL expiration and DNS propagation windows during live infrastructure updates.</li>\n        <li><strong>OpSec Failure via Direct Querying:</strong> Sending active queries from identifiable corporate IP addresses rather than isolated research proxies.</li>\n        <li><strong>Failure to Preserve Evidence:</strong> Proceeding with case analysis without first hashing and timestamping raw data.</li>\n        <li><strong>Confirmation Bias:</strong> Selectively gathering records that support a preconceived attribution hypothesis while ignoring contradictory technical evidence.</li>\n        <li><strong>Context Blindness:</strong> Failing to understand the business model of the target organization, leading to misinterpretation of normal multi-cloud routing as malicious activity.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h3>4. Master Pre-Flight & Post-Investigation Operational Checklist</h3>\n      <p>Before launching an investigation and before publishing final intelligence reports, complete the following operational checklist:</p>\n      <table><tr><th>Task</th><th>Status</th></tr><tr><td>Sherlock scan complete</td><td>[✓]</td></tr></table>\n\n      <h3>5. Final Capstone Real-World Scenario Walkthrough</h3>\n      <p>Complete threat actor persona unmasking across 30+ platforms using Sherlock.</p>\n\n      <h3>6. Comprehensive Mastery Knowledge Assessment (12 Questions)</h3>\n      <p>Test your comprehensive understanding of Sherlock across theoretical concepts, command syntax, output interpretation, cross-tool correlation, and evidence forensics:</p>\n      <ol><li><strong>Q1:</strong> What file contains Sherlock's site signature database? <em>(Answer: data.json).</em></li></ol>\n    ",
